<ns0:para xmlns:ns0="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:ns1="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="fs-id1167833054733">The next function whose graph we will look at is called the constant function and its equation is of the form <ns1:math><ns1:mrow><ns1:mi>f</ns1:mi><ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>(</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>x</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>)</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>=</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>b</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>,</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow></ns1:math> where <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">b</ns0:emphasis> is any real number. If we replace the <ns1:math><ns1:mrow><ns1:mi>f</ns1:mi><ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>(</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>x</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>)</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow></ns1:mrow></ns1:math> with y, we get <ns1:math><ns1:mrow><ns1:mi>y</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>=</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>b</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>.</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow></ns1:math> We recognize this as the horizontal line whose <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">y</ns0:emphasis>-intercept is <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">b</ns0:emphasis>. The graph of the function <ns1:math><ns1:mrow><ns1:mi>f</ns1:mi><ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>(</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>x</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>)</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>=</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>b</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>,</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow></ns1:math> is also the horizontal line whose <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">y</ns0:emphasis>-intercept is <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">b</ns0:emphasis>.</ns0:para>
